¶
'''barter''' (bär′tėr), ''v.'' [< late ME. ''bartren'' for *''barten'', *''bareten'' (the inserted ''r'' being due perhaps to the suffix of the OF. infinitive, or to dependence on the noun ''barator'', ''bareter'', etc.: see ''barrator''), < OF. ''barēter'', ''barater'', barter, truck, cheat, < ''barat'', ''barate'', ''barete'', barter, cheating: see ''barrat''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To traffic or trade by exchanging one commodity for another, in distinction from buying and selling for money.

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To give (one thing or commodity) for another of equivalent or supposed equivalent value: ''with'' a person, ''for'' (formerly ''with'') a thing: as, to ''barter'' one’s jewels ''for'' bread.
{{smaller block|As my faith has once been given to you, I never will ''barter'' it ''with'' another.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, v. 1.}}
Rude people who were willing to ''barter'' costly furs ''for'' trifles.
{{right|''Bancroft'', Hist. U. S., I. 91.}}}}
'''2.''' To exchange, in general.—'''To barter away''', {{smaller|to dispose of by barter, especially in an unwise or dishonorable way; bargain away: as, ''to barter away'' human rights for the patronage of the great.}}
{{smaller block|He also ''bartered away'' plums . . . for nuts.
{{right|''Locke.''}}}}

'''barter''' (bär′tėr), ''n.'' [< ''barter'', ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of exchanging; specifically, the act or practice of trafficking by exchange of commodities.
{{smaller block|All government, indeed every human benefit and enjoyment, every virtue, and every prudent act, is founded on compromise and ''barter''.
{{right|''Burke'', Conciliation with America, 1775.}}
Article is exchanged for article without the use of money or credit. This is simple ''barter''.
{{right|''D. Webster'', Speech, Senate, March 18, 1834.}}}}

